---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

// V-713 — public security overview. Pitched at customer security
// review + procurement teams. Companion to /docs/data-residency,
// /docs/audit-log, /docs/incident-policy. Keeps the surface area
// honest — describes what we do, what we don't, and where to find
// the auditable evidence.
---

<BaseLayout title="Security overview — Driftstack" description="Driftstack's security posture: data handling, encryption, auth, incident response, and what's in scope for our threat model.">
  <main class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-12 prose prose-slate dark:prose-invert">
    <h1>Security overview</h1>

    <p class="lead">
      This page is the entry point for a security review of
      Driftstack. It links out to the concrete controls + policy
      pages; this document is a map, not an exhaustive specification.
      For the contract-grade detail (SOC2 reports, pen-test
      summaries) reach out to
      <a href="mailto:security@driftstack.dev">security@driftstack.dev</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Data handling</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>At rest:</strong> All customer data in Postgres is on
        encrypted volumes (AES-256). API keys, OAuth client secrets,
        and MFA seeds are stored as SHA-256 hashes — the plaintext is
        only ever shown once, at mint time.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>In transit:</strong> TLS 1.2+ enforced on every
        public endpoint. HSTS preload-listed for
        <code>driftstack.dev</code> and subdomains.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Recordings:</strong> WebM files land in Cloudflare R2
        with server-side encryption. Playback URLs are presigned and
        expire in 1 hour; the underlying object is never publicly
        listable.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Profile state:</strong> Browser cookies, localStorage,
        and IndexedDB persisted per profile are encrypted at the
        application layer with a per-account key (V-312); the raw
        Postgres column never holds plaintext.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Authentication + authorization</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Customer keys are scoped: <code>read</code> /
        <code>write</code> / <code>account_owner</code>. We default to
        least-privilege; the dashboard's "create key" flow defaults to
        <code>read</code> with an explicit checkbox to widen.
      </li>
      <li>
        MFA (TOTP) is available for every account and required for
        any operation we classify as "sensitive" — see the dashboard
        for the per-account toggle. We re-prompt for MFA after 15
        minutes of step-up inactivity.
      </li>
      <li>
        Admin actions are gated behind a separate
        <code>driftstack_internal_admin</code> scope that no customer
        key can hold. See <a href="/docs/admin-api">/docs/admin-api</a>
        for the surface.
      </li>
      <li>
        Every login event + every key mint/revoke is captured in the
        account audit log (V-074); customers can self-serve a full
        log export.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Network + infrastructure</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Stateless API services in three regions (EU, US-East,
        AP-South). Customer traffic is GeoDNS-routed to the nearest
        region; cross-region failover is automatic for the API +
        dashboard surfaces.
      </li>
      <li>
        Postgres clusters per region with continuous WAL archiving to
        R2; 7-day point-in-time recovery is the default. Enterprise
        contracts can negotiate longer windows.
      </li>
      <li>
        No customer data is ever copied across regions without a
        customer-driven request. See
        <a href="/docs/data-residency">/docs/data-residency</a> for
        the residency policy.
      </li>
      <li>
        WAF + rate-limiting in front of every public endpoint. DDoS
        absorption is handled at the CDN edge.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Browser sandbox</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Customer-supplied <code>script</code> bodies run in a
        sandboxed Node context with no filesystem, network, or
        process API. Only the <code>page</code> automation surface +
        <code>console</code> are exposed. CPU + wall-clock budgets
        are enforced per session.
      </li>
      <li>
        Each session is one isolated browser instance backed by an
        ephemeral disk slice; cross-session state never bleeds. The
        only persistence path is the customer-managed
        <strong>profile</strong> mechanism (which we encrypt — see
        above).
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Sub-processors</h2>
    <p>
      A full sub-processor list with a description of what each
      receives is available at
      <a href="/legal/sub-processors">/legal/sub-processors</a>. The
      shortlist as of 2026:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Stripe (card billing only — no payment data touches our infra)</li>
      <li>NowPayments (crypto checkout)</li>
      <li>Cloudflare (CDN, WAF, R2 object storage)</li>
      <li>Postmark (transactional email)</li>
      <li>Sentry (engineering error monitoring; PII-scrubbed at SDK level)</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      We publish 30-day notice before adding or rotating a sub-
      processor. Enterprise contracts can opt into the announcement
      mailing list at <code>announcements@</code>.
    </p>

    <h2>Audit + observability</h2>
    <p>
      Three log streams are customer-readable:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href="/docs/audit-log">Account audit log</a> — every
        mutation on your account (key mints, profile changes, billing
        events).
      </li>
      <li>
        Session logs — per-session navigation + console output
        retained per tier.
      </li>
      <li>
        Cost ledger — every billable event, queryable via the API.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Incident response</h2>
    <p>
      See <a href="/docs/incident-policy">/docs/incident-policy</a>
      for the disclosure timeline + the
      <a href="https://status.driftstack.dev">status page</a>
      cadence. Security-relevant incidents are disclosed within 72h
      of confirmation; we do not bury exposure events.
    </p>

    <h2>Vulnerability reporting</h2>
    <p>
      Email <a href="mailto:security@driftstack.dev">security@driftstack.dev</a>
      with the details. We respond within 1 business day. Our
      <a href="/legal/vulnerability-disclosure">vulnerability
      disclosure policy</a> covers safe-harbour for good-faith
      research; please review it before testing.
    </p>

    <h2>Related</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/docs/data-residency">Data residency</a></li>
      <li><a href="/docs/admin-api">Admin API + scope</a></li>
      <li><a href="/docs/incident-policy">Incident policy</a></li>
      <li><a href="/docs/audit-log">Audit log</a></li>
      <li><a href="/legal/sub-processors">Sub-processors</a></li>
    </ul>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
